<td id="S3.T2.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><math id="S3.T2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="25" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>25</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">25</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S3.T2.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><math id="S3.T2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="24" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>24</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">24</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S3.T2.2.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><math id="S3.T2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S3.T2.2.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><math id="S3.T2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S3.T2.2.2.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><math id="S3.T2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S3.T2.2.2.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><math id="S3.T2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S3.T2.2.2.8" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><math id="S3.T2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S3.T2.2.2.9" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><math id="S3.T2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>Nested-CV layer selection: how often each layer is chosen, across all <math id="S3.T2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> values, as part of the best (layer, <math id="S3.T2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>) configuration over the 50 outer splits. The grid search operates jointly over layer and <math id="S3.T2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>; this table shows the layer counts collapsed across <math id="S3.T2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> to make the layer preference visible. Selection concentrates on layers 29–30, accounting for <math id="S3.T2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="49" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>49</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">49</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.T2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>50</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50</annotation></semantics></math> splits, with deeper layers never selected; within these, <math id="S3.T2.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=0.01</annotation></semantics></math> is the most frequent regularization strength, appearing in <math id="S3.T2.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="43" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>43</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">43</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.T2.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>50</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50</annotation></semantics></math> splits. The full procedure’s leakage-free outer-test AUC is <math id="S3.T2.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.931\pm 0.008" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.931</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.008</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.931\pm 0.008</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Results</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Each number in Table <a href="#S3.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 3.3 Results ‣ 3 Correctness is linearly readable before generation ‣ Code Correctness Signals in LLM Hidden States: Pre-Generation Probing and Repair Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> is a leakage-free procedure-level estimate: for the raw and residualized probes, layer and <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> are jointly selected inside each outer training fold and evaluated on the untouched outer-test fold; for the prompt-only baseline, only <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is selected as there is no layer. Reporting procedure-level numbers for all three configurations ensures they are directly comparable, since each reflects the performance of the full select-then-fit pipeline applied consistently, rather than locking any configuration to a specific hyperparameter value chosen by inspecting the test set.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T3" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S3.T3.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S3.T3.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T3.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt">Configuration</th>
<th id="S3.T3.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">Test AUC (mean <math id="S3.T3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> 95% CI)</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S3.T3.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T3.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">Raw hidden states</th>
<td id="S3.T3.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S3.T3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.931\pm 0.008" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.931</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.008</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.931\pm 0.008</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T3.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T3.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Residualized</th>
<td id="S3.T3.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.T3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.911\pm 0.010" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.911</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.010</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.911\pm 0.010</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T3.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T3.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb">Prompt-only baseline</th>
<td id="S3.T3.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><math id="S3.T3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.754\pm 0.014" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.754</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.014</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.754\pm 0.014</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span>Nested CV procedure-level performance for the three probe configurations, averaged over 50 outer stratified <math id="S3.T3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="80/20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>80</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">80/20</annotation></semantics></math> splits. Values are leakage-free outer-test AUCs.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The raw configuration reaches mean outer-test AUC <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.931" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.931</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.931</annotation></semantics></math>. After linearly removing prompt length from every hidden state dimension, the residualized configuration reaches <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.911" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.911</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.911</annotation></semantics></math>. The prompt-only baseline reaches <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.754" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.754</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.754</annotation></semantics></math>. The gap between the residualized probe and the prompt-only baseline is about <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>16</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16</annotation></semantics></math> AUC points, placing a concrete floor under the result: a substantial portion of the hidden state’s pass/fail signal is not explained by prompt length and survives its removal. The signal is present before a single output token is generated, encoded in the model’s internal representation of the prompt alone.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The prompt-only baseline reported above uses logistic regression. To check that this floor does not depend on the choice of a linear model, a random forest, a gradient boosting model, and a small multilayer perceptron were each fit on <span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">prompt_tokens</span> alone under the same nested CV procedure described in Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Layer landscape and selection ‣ 3 Correctness is linearly readable before generation ‣ Code Correctness Signals in LLM Hidden States: Pre-Generation Probing and Repair Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>. Table <a href="#S3.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 3.3 Results ‣ 3 Correctness is linearly readable before generation ‣ Code Correctness Signals in LLM Hidden States: Pre-Generation Probing and Repair Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> reports their mean outer-test AUC. None exceeds the logistic baseline, and all remain near <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.754" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.754</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.754</annotation></semantics></math>, far below the residualized probe at <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.911" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.911</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.911</annotation></semantics></math>. Prompt length alone does not approach the residualized probe, so the probe’s gap over the prompt-length baseline is not an artifact of the baseline being restricted to a linear model.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T4" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S3.T4.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S3.T4.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T4.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt">Prompt-only model</th>
<th id="S3.T4.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">Test AUC (mean <math id="S3.T4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> 95% CI)</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S3.T4.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T4.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">Logistic regression</th>
<td id="S3.T4.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S3.T4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.754\pm 0.014" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.754</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.014</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.754\pm 0.014</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T4.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T4.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Random forest</th>
<td id="S3.T4.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.T4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.735\pm 0.016" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.735</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.016</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.735\pm 0.016</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T4.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T4.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Gradient boosting</th>
<td id="S3.T4.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.T4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.736\pm 0.016" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.736</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.016</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.736\pm 0.016</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T4.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T4.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb">Multilayer perceptron</th>
<td id="S3.T4.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><math id="S3.T4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.753\pm 0.014" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.753</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.014</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.753\pm 0.014</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 4: </span>Mean outer-test AUC for prompt-only models, each fit on <span id="S3.T4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">prompt_tokens</span> alone following the nested CV procedure of Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Layer landscape and selection ‣ 3 Correctness is linearly readable before generation ‣ Code Correctness Signals in LLM Hidden States: Pre-Generation Probing and Repair Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, with model-specific hyperparameter grids tuned in the inner folds. The logistic model is the strongest, and the nonlinear families do not improve on it.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>A Geometric Direction of Repair Success</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When the model is given the chance to repair its own failing code, the question is whether the shift in its hidden state between the failed attempt and the repair attempt carries a stable, decodable signature of whether the repair will succeed. The headline answer of this section is that a statistically detectable geometric direction does exist in the raw deltas, and is statistically significant on two complementary tests, but does not survive a conditional residualization once observable repair-context covariates are checked and found to differ between the success and failure groups.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For each task where attempt 0 fails the unit tests and attempt 1 exists, the per-task hidden state delta is defined as</p>
<table id="S4.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{i}=h_{\text{layer 29}}(\text{attempt 1}_{i})-h_{\text{layer 29}}(\text{attempt 0}_{i})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mtext>layer 29</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mtext>attempt 1</mtext><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mtext>layer 29</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mtext>attempt 0</mtext><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{i}=h_{\text{layer 29}}(\text{attempt 1}_{i})-h_{\text{layer 29}}(\text{attempt 0}_{i}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p2.2" class="ltx_p">and the candidate contrastive direction is</p>
<table id="S4.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v=\mathrm{mean}\bigl(\{\delta_{i}:i\in\text{success}\}\bigr)-\mathrm{mean}\bigl(\{\delta_{i}:i\in\text{failure}\}\bigr)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>mean</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mtext>success</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>mean</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mtext>failure</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v=\mathrm{mean}\bigl(\{\delta_{i}:i\in\text{success}\}\bigr)-\mathrm{mean}\bigl(\{\delta_{i}:i\in\text{failure}\}\bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where success and failure refer to whether attempt 1 passes or fails the unit tests. Each attempt is a fresh forward pass on the full prompt with no state carried over from previous attempts, as described in Section <a href="#S2" title="2 Setup ‣ Code Correctness Signals in LLM Hidden States: Pre-Generation Probing and Repair Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, so <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is driven entirely by what the model re-reads on attempt 1: the failing code from attempt 0, the captured error output, and the retry instruction.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Data cleaning.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Two filters are applied before computing deltas, both targeting the same underlying pathology, model generations that hit the 768-token cap with repetitive broken code, at two different scopes: trajectory-wide and attempt-0 only. The first filter removes the 47 tasks tagged as repetition-loop tasks, where the model is stuck producing broken output that hits the 768-token cap across all five attempts so the entire trajectory is contaminated. After this initial pass, 327 candidate 0<math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math>1 transitions remain. Of these, a further 91 are excluded because attempt 0 alone hit the same cap: even when attempt 1 produced valid code, the truncated attempt-0 generation is pasted verbatim into the repair prompt and contaminates the delta for the failure group, with 83 of the 91 failing at attempt 1. The final clean sample contains 236 transitions, 128 with a successful repair and 108 with a failed repair.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Raw tests</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Two complementary tests are run on the contrastive direction <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> in the full 2560-dimensional layer-29 space. Both compare the real statistic to an empirical null distribution generated by repeatedly shuffling the pass/fail labels across the 236 deltas.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The first test, on magnitude, asks whether the success/failure mean difference is larger than label noise can explain. The norm <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|v\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|v\|</annotation></semantics></math> is computed on the real assignment and on 1000 random shufflings of the labels. The second test, on inclination, asks whether the direction is geometrically stable: the 236 deltas are split into two stratified halves (each preserving the 128/108 success/failure ratio), <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> is recomputed independently on each half, and the cosine similarity between the two recovered directions is taken. This is repeated 1000 times. A null distribution is generated by the same split-half procedure on shuffled-label data. Results for both tests are reported in Table <a href="#S4.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 4.1 Raw tests ‣ 4 A Geometric Direction of Repair Success ‣ Code Correctness Signals in LLM Hidden States: Pre-Generation Probing and Repair Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T5" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S4.T5.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S4.T5.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T5.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt">Test</th>
<th id="S4.T5.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">Real value</th>
<th id="S4.T5.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">Null mean</th>
<th id="S4.T5.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">p-value</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T5.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T5.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">Magnitude <math id="S4.T5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|v\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|v\|</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="S4.T5.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S4.T5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9.76" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>9.76</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9.76</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.T5.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S4.T5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3.64</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.64</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.T5.2.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S4.T5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;0.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;0.001</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T5.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T5.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb">Split-half cosine</th>
<td id="S4.T5.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><math id="S4.T5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.767" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.767</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.767</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.T5.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><math id="S4.T5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-0.011" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.011</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-0.011</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.T5.2.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><math id="S4.T5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;0.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;0.001</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 5: </span>Two complementary tests of the contrastive direction <math id="S4.T5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> on the 236 clean <math id="S4.T5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to 1</annotation></semantics></math> transitions. The magnitude null has std <math id="S4.T5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.59" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.59</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.59</annotation></semantics></math> and max <math id="S4.T5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6.58" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6.58</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6.58</annotation></semantics></math>; the real magnitude is therefore roughly ten standard deviations above the null mean, and 0 of 1000 shuffles reached it. The split-half cosine null has mean <math id="S4.T5.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-0.011" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.011</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-0.011</annotation></semantics></math> and std <math id="S4.T5.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.225" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.225</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.225</annotation></semantics></math>; the real mean cosine of <math id="S4.T5.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.767" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.767</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.767</annotation></semantics></math> is therefore over three standard deviations above the null mean.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Taken on their own, these two tests look like clean evidence for a repair-relevant feature: the magnitude sits roughly ten standard deviations above the null mean and is larger than every one of the 1000 shuffled norms, and the inclination is moderately stable across random data splits. The qualifier is introduced in the next subsection.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Sensitivity check</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The contrastive construction <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{mean}(\text{success})-\mathrm{mean}(\text{failure})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>mean</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>success</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>mean</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>failure</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{mean}(\text{success})-\mathrm{mean}(\text{failure})</annotation></semantics></math> is a difference of group means, so it already cancels the linear contribution of any covariate whose mean is equal across the two groups. For a one-hot-encoded categorical covariate this condition is equivalent to its category proportions being equal across groups, that is, the same categorical distribution. Residualization is therefore necessary only when a candidate covariate’s group means differ. Otherwise the operation removes within-group variance without changing between-group means and collapses the signal artifactually. The principled procedure is to test, before residualizing, whether each candidate covariate differs between the success and failure groups, and to include in the residualization only those that do.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Three candidate covariates are computed per delta from the attempt-level log:</p>
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\texttt{prompt\_tokens}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">prompt_tokens</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\texttt{prompt\_tokens}</annotation></semantics></math>, the change in input token length between attempt 1 and attempt 0.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_typewriter">code_length_attempt_0</span>, the number of completion tokens produced at attempt 0, used as a proxy for how much wrong code gets pasted into the repair prompt.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_typewriter">failure_type</span> of attempt 0, a categorical with three observed levels (assertion, runtime, syntax). Of the five non-pass labels in the taxonomy described in Section <a href="#S2" title="2 Setup ‣ Code Correctness Signals in LLM Hidden States: Pre-Generation Probing and Repair Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, only assertion, runtime, and syntax appear in the clean sample; timeout and the unknown fallback are absent.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Welch’s two-sample t-test, which does not assume equal variance, is used for the two continuous covariates and a chi-squared test on the <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\times 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times 2</annotation></semantics></math> contingency table is used for the categorical covariate. Each test produces a p-value reported in Table <a href="#S4.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ 4.2 Sensitivity check ‣ 4 A Geometric Direction of Repair Success ‣ Code Correctness Signals in LLM Hidden States: Pre-Generation Probing and Repair Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>. The null hypothesis is that the success and failure group means are equal for the two continuous covariates, and that the failure-type proportions are equal across the two groups for the categorical covariate.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T6" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S4.T6.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S4.T6.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T6.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt">Covariate</th>
<th id="S4.T6.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">Success mean (n=128)</th>
<th id="S4.T6.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">Failure mean (n=108)</th>
<th id="S4.T6.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">p-value</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T6.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T6.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t"><math id="S4.T6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\texttt{prompt\_tokens}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">prompt_tokens</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\texttt{prompt\_tokens}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="S4.T6.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S4.T6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="337.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>337.1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">337.1</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.T6.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S4.T6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="431.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>431.5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">431.5</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.T6.2.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S4.T6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;0.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;0.001</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T6.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T6.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row"><span id="S4.T6.2.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_typewriter">code_length_attempt_0</span></th>
<td id="S4.T6.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.T6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="188.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>188.8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">188.8</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.T6.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.T6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="290.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>290.9</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">290.9</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.T6.2.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.T6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;0.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;0.001</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T6.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T6.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb"><span id="S4.T6.2.4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_typewriter">failure_type</span> of attempt 0</th>
<td id="S4.T6.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" colspan="2">(categorical, <math id="S4.T6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> levels)</td>
<td id="S4.T6.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><math id="S4.T6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;0.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;0.001</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 6: </span>Covariate balance check on the 236 clean 0<math id="S4.T6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math>1 transitions. All three observable covariates differ significantly between the success and failure groups, which warrants residualizing against them.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">All three covariates differ at <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;0.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;0.001</annotation></semantics></math>. Successfully repaired tasks tend to come from shorter attempt-0 code, from a shorter prompt growth, and from a different mix of failure types (more runtime errors, fewer assertion errors). The contrastive subtraction cancels only the linear contribution of covariates whose group means are equal. Since the group means of all three differ, the subtraction does not cancel them, and residualization against them is conceptually warranted.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The operation is now described formally. Let <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in\mathbb{R}^{236\times 4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>236</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in\mathbb{R}^{236\times 4}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the covariate matrix with one row per task and four columns (the two continuous covariates and the two failure-type dummies, with assertion as the baseline level). For each of the 2560 hidden state dimensions <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, an OLS regression is fit on the 236 deltas with the <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-th coordinate as response and <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> as predictor matrix, producing an intercept <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\alpha}^{(d)}\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\alpha}^{(d)}\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> and a slope vector <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\beta}^{(d)}\in\mathbb{R}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>β</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\beta}^{(d)}\in\mathbb{R}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math>. The residualized delta for task <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> in dimension <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is then <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\delta}_{i,d}=\delta_{i,d}-\bigl(\hat{\alpha}^{(d)}+X_{i}\hat{\beta}^{(d)}\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>δ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>β</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\delta}_{i,d}=\delta_{i,d}-\bigl(\hat{\alpha}^{(d)}+X_{i}\hat{\beta}^{(d)}\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the covariate row for task <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>. Stacking across all 2560 dimensions yields a residualized delta matrix of the same shape as the original. The contrastive direction <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> is recomputed on the residualized deltas, and the two tests are rerun. Table <a href="#S4.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ 4.2 Sensitivity check ‣ 4 A Geometric Direction of Repair Success ‣ Code Correctness Signals in LLM Hidden States: Pre-Generation Probing and Repair Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> reports the raw and residualized statistics side by side.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T7" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S4.T7.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S4.T7.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T7.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt">Statistic</th>
<th id="S4.T7.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">Raw</th>
<th id="S4.T7.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">Residualized</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T7.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T7.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">Magnitude <math id="S4.T7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|v\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|v\|</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="S4.T7.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S4.T7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9.76" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>9.76</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9.76</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.T7.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S4.T7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.72" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3.72</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.72</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T7.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T7.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Magnitude p-value</th>
<td id="S4.T7.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.T7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;0.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;0.001</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.T7.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.T7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.126" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.126</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.126</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T7.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T7.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Split-half cosine</th>
<td id="S4.T7.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.T7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.767" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.767</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.767</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.T7.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.T7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.140" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.140</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.140</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T7.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T7.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb">Split-half p-value</th>
<td id="S4.T7.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><math id="S4.T7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;0.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;0.001</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.T7.2.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><math id="S4.T7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.942" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.942</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.942</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 7: </span>Raw versus conditional residualization on the 236 clean <math id="S4.T7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to 1</annotation></semantics></math> transitions. After residualizing against the three observable covariates that differ between groups, neither the magnitude test nor the split half test reaches significance.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The signal does not survive the full conditional residualization. The residualized norm of <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.72" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3.72</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.72</annotation></semantics></math> retains only <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="38.1\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>38.1</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">38.1\%</annotation></semantics></math> of the raw norm <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9.76" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>9.76</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9.76</annotation></semantics></math>, the split-half cosine drops from <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.767" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.767</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.767</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.140" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.140</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.140</annotation></semantics></math>, and neither the magnitude test (<math id="S4.SS2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.126" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.126</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.126</annotation></semantics></math>) nor the split-half test (<math id="S4.SS2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.942" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.942</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.942</annotation></semantics></math>) reaches significance. This should not be read as the raw signal being false: the raw direction passes both statistical tests by wide margins. The honest reading is that the observed geometric direction is a correlate of repair success, substantially mediated by what differs in the repair context, rather than evidence of an isolated repair-comprehension direction in activation space.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Two of the three covariates used in the residualization are partially redundant: <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\texttt{prompt\_tokens}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">prompt_tokens</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\texttt{prompt\_tokens}</annotation></semantics></math> decomposes into the failed code length, the error output length, and an instruction template that is constant up to a single <span id="S4.SS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">failure_type</span> token (the <span id="S4.SS2.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">failure_type</span> variation is one of the three covariates already included in the residualization matrix, so its contribution is subtracted explicitly), so the failed code length is also present in <span id="S4.SS2.p7.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_typewriter">code_length_attempt_0</span>. Empirically, the two are nearly collinear, with a Pearson <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.995" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.995</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.995</annotation></semantics></math>. An extra regression column, however, absorbs on average about <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/n</annotation></semantics></math> of the variance even when it carries no real information, and the redundant <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\texttt{prompt\_tokens}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">prompt_tokens</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\texttt{prompt\_tokens}</annotation></semantics></math> column is a single parameter against 236 deltas, so its spurious contribution to the in-sample <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> of the per-dimension OLS fit is about 0.4 percentage points. The measured value agrees: adding the redundant column on top of the other three raises the mean in-sample <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> by under one percentage point. The residualization therefore removes essentially the same variance whether the redundant column is included or not, and any overfitting bias on the residualized norm is negligible.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Beyond the first attempt</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The magnitude and inclination tests above are estimated on the <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to 1</annotation></semantics></math> transition only, where 128 successes and 108 failures provide enough data for the contrastive construction. At later transitions the situation changes sharply, as Table <a href="#S4.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ 4.3 Beyond the first attempt ‣ 4 A Geometric Direction of Repair Success ‣ Code Correctness Signals in LLM Hidden States: Pre-Generation Probing and Repair Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> shows.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T8" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S4.T8.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S4.T8.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T8.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt">Attempt</th>
<th id="S4.T8.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">Pass rate</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T8.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T8.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">0</th>
<td id="S4.T8.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">15.8%</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T8.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T8.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row">1</th>
<td id="S4.T8.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">36.4%</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T8.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T8.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row">2</th>
<td id="S4.T8.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">6.7%</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T8.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T8.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row">3</th>
<td id="S4.T8.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">2.7%</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T8.2.6" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T8.2.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb">4</th>
<td id="S4.T8.2.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">1.4%</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 8: </span>Per-attempt pass rates among tasks that reached each attempt (the denominator shrinks across rows as tasks exit the trajectory after passing). The bulk of the gain happens at attempt 1; later attempts contribute only marginally.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The first piece of error feedback delivers most of the information the model can use, and subsequent retries yield diminishing returns. This is consistent with prior reports that iterative self-repair plateaus after the first one or two attempts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>. At transitions <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\to 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\to 2</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\to 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\to 3</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\to 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\to 4</annotation></semantics></math> there are only <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="13" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>13</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">13</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> successful repairs respectively, which is too few for any contrastive direction to be estimated regardless of whether one exists. Extending the geometric analysis of Sections <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Raw tests ‣ 4 A Geometric Direction of Repair Success ‣ Code Correctness Signals in LLM Hidden States: Pre-Generation Probing and Repair Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> and <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Sensitivity check ‣ 4 A Geometric Direction of Repair Success ‣ Code Correctness Signals in LLM Hidden States: Pre-Generation Probing and Repair Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> to later steps would require a substantially larger dataset with many more successful multi-step repairs.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Limitations and further null results</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The following reports the constraints of the study and two additional trajectory-level analyses whose outcomes do not support clean claims either way. For these analyses, the distinction between "the signal does not exist" and "the data were not sufficient to detect it" cannot be resolved with the present dataset.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S5.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Single model.</span> All experiments use Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507, run locally on a single GPU. Replicating across model families would require substantially more inference compute and time; this is left for future work.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Captured layer range.</span> The probe AUC peaks at the shallowest captured layer, layer 29, with layer 30 statistically indistinguishable. Because the peak sits at the lower edge of the captured range rather than in its interior, a layer below 29 cannot be ruled out as comparable or better. The repair-direction conclusion is, however, stable across the captured range: re-running the Section <a href="#S4" title="4 A Geometric Direction of Repair Success ‣ Code Correctness Signals in LLM Hidden States: Pre-Generation Probing and Repair Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> analysis at layers 29, 30, and 35 yields the same verdict, making it unlikely, though unproven, that a nearby lower layer would change it.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Data starvation at later repair transitions.</span> The <math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\to 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\to 2</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\to 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\to 3</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\to 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\to 4</annotation></semantics></math> transitions have only <math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="13" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>13</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">13</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> successful repairs respectively. No contrastive direction can be meaningfully estimated at those steps, so trajectory-level claims beyond the <math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to 1</annotation></semantics></math> transition are out of scope.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Covariate-set composition.</span> The conditional residualization in Section <a href="#S4" title="4 A Geometric Direction of Repair Success ‣ Code Correctness Signals in LLM Hidden States: Pre-Generation Probing and Repair Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> uses two continuous covariates that share information: <math id="S5.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\texttt{prompt\_tokens}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">prompt_tokens</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\texttt{prompt\_tokens}</annotation></semantics></math> contains <span id="S5.I1.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_typewriter">code_length_attempt_0</span>, with Pearson <math id="S5.I1.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=0.995" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.995</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=0.995</annotation></semantics></math>. The redundant covariate is a single parameter against 236 data points, so the resulting overfitting bias is negligible; see the disclosure paragraph at the end of Section <a href="#S4" title="4 A Geometric Direction of Repair Success ‣ Code Correctness Signals in LLM Hidden States: Pre-Generation Probing and Repair Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> for the empirical justification. A cleaner covariate set would avoid the overlap, but it could not be computed from the retained logs without re-running inference on all 444 tasks, which was beyond the compute available for this study.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Directional consistency across repair steps.</span> The cosine between <math id="S5.I1.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{t\to t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{t\to t+1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.I1.i5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{t+1\to t+2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{t+1\to t+2}</annotation></semantics></math> within the same trajectory averages to approximately <math id="S5.I1.i5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-0.16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-0.16</annotation></semantics></math> for eventually-passing tasks and <math id="S5.I1.i5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-0.25" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.25</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-0.25</annotation></semantics></math> for eventually-failing tasks, slightly anti-correlated in both cases. With so few successful repairs at later transitions, it is not possible to distinguish a genuine absence of directional consistency from insufficient data.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Distance to the success region.</span> Tracking the distance from each attempt’s hidden state to the centroid of attempt-0 passing tasks, the “success region”, shows no monotonic convergence: the distance curves for eventually-passing and eventually-failing tasks track each other closely from attempt 1 onward, with neither approaching the success region.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This work examined two related questions about the layer-29 hidden states of Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507 on LiveCodeBench tasks. The first asked whether code correctness is decodable from the prompt-final hidden state, before any token is generated. A linear probe answered yes, with performance robust across many random splits and surviving the linear removal of prompt length. Prompt length alone, fit with nonlinear models as well as a linear one, stayed near <math id="S6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.754" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.754</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.754</annotation></semantics></math> and far below the probe, so its lead is not an artifact of a linear baseline. The second asked whether the hidden state shift from a failing attempt to its repair attempt carries an isolable geometric signature of repair success. A statistically detectable contrastive direction exists in the raw deltas, but it does not survive a conditional residualization once observable repair-context covariates are checked and found to differ between groups. The direction is best read as a correlate of repair success mediated by the structure of the repair context, not as an isolated repair-comprehension feature.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Both questions in this paper were answered with the help of the same tool: residualization, the operation of regressing each hidden state dimension on a candidate covariate and subtracting the prediction, applied before the downstream test. In Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Correctness is linearly readable before generation ‣ Code Correctness Signals in LLM Hidden States: Pre-Generation Probing and Repair Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> the probe’s signal survived residualizing against prompt length, showing that the hidden states carry a correctness signal not reducible to input length. In Section <a href="#S4" title="4 A Geometric Direction of Repair Success ‣ Code Correctness Signals in LLM Hidden States: Pre-Generation Probing and Repair Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> the same operation, this time against three repair-context covariates that differed between groups, collapsed the geometric direction. What looked like an isolated repair-comprehension feature in the raw deltas was primarily driven by what differed in the repair prompt itself. The same diagnostic told a different story in each case, and that is the point: residualization is informative because it can give either verdict. In Section <a href="#S4" title="4 A Geometric Direction of Repair Success ‣ Code Correctness Signals in LLM Hidden States: Pre-Generation Probing and Repair Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> this informativeness was made conditional on whether the covariate to be removed is one that the contrastive construction has not already cancelled, which is what motivated the covariate-distribution check used there.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Two extensions of this work are natural. The first is to replicate both analyses across other open-weight models, which would help separate findings that are specific to this one model from properties shared across language models. The second, more targeted at the repair-direction analysis, is to collect a larger sample of successful multi-step repairs: with more successful transitions at <math id="S6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\to 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\to 2</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\to 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\to 3</annotation></semantics></math>, and beyond, the contrastive direction analysis could be repeated step by step rather than only at the first repair. Code generation is a particularly tractable domain for this kind of analysis: outputs are objectively verifiable, the failure modes are observable, and each task gives a binary pass/fail signal. More generally, as language models grow more capable their internal computation grows more elaborate, and there is correspondingly more to learn from studying what those internals encode. The contribution of this study is as much methodological as empirical: a single confound-control procedure that upheld one claim and overturned the other, applied honestly to both.</p>
</div>
</section>
